Procedures: what can be called, and calling it (RFC 05).

```console
$ zenctl service --help
Procedures on the `@rpc` plane

Usage: zenctl service <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list  List registered procedures (bus-served slices, or `--registry`)
  info  One producer's `@rpc` surface, with the key shape a call would use
  call  Call a procedure (on-bus)
  help  Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help

$ zenctl service list --help
List registered procedures (bus-served slices, or `--registry`)

Usage: zenctl service list [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --producer <PRODUCER>
          Only this producer

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl service info --help
One producer's `@rpc` surface, with the key shape a call would use.

`service list` says what exists across the fleet; this says what one producer offers and how to reach it — the question left over, and the one you have immediately before `service call`.

Usage: zenctl service info [OPTIONS] <PRODUCER> [PROCEDURE]

Arguments:
  <PRODUCER>
          Producer name

  [PROCEDURE]
          Only this procedure path, e.g. `introspect`

Options:
      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

$ zenctl service call --help
Call a procedure (on-bus)

Usage: zenctl service call [OPTIONS] <ORIGIN> <PRODUCER> <PROCEDURE>

Arguments:
  <ORIGIN>
          Origin to target: a host id (`h-3fa9c2d41b7e`), `*` for the whole fleet, or `@catalog` for a service origin

  <PRODUCER>
          Producer name. Omit for a service origin, which has no producer chunk

  <PROCEDURE>
          Procedure path, e.g. `introspect` or `artifact/status`

Options:
      --param <K=V>
          Selector parameters, repeatable: `--param state=established`

      --body <TEXT|@FILE|->
          Request body: inline JSON, `@file`, or `-` for stdin

      --attachment <TEXT|@FILE|->
          Attachment riding beside the request, verbatim — never schema-encoded (#117's rule, on the call side: #126). Inline text, `@file`, or `-` for stdin

      --no-validate
          Skip the registry lookup (and with it the registry-layer forbidden-fanout refusal and any body validation)

      --raw
          Send the request body verbatim: no schema lookup, no encoding

      --base <BASE>
          The deployment base — the first chunk(s) of every key on the wire.
          
          Applications set this as their Zenoh session `namespace` and never spell it. `zenctl` deliberately does **not**: a debug tool runs un-namespaced so it sees the wire as it really is, including traffic from outside the deployment (RFC 09 §5) — which is what lets it spot a leak. So it has to be told what the base is. Resolution: flag > env > active context (`zenctl context …`) > empty — the base-less bus-root deployment, the RFC v1.6 default, whose wire keys start at `v1/`. `zenctl base list` discovers the bases in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

      --registry <DIR>
          Local registry directory (`registry/*.toml`), repeatable. When given, registry-sourced commands answer offline from these files instead of the live bus

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`)

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting.
          
          OFF by default, and you should think before turning it on: a scouting explorer joins whatever mesh it can find, which is how a throwaway session ends up talking to a production fleet.

      --timeout <TIMEOUT>
          Seconds to wait for replies / to watch the bus (default 5; a context may override the default)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file (#122): the passthrough that reaches a secured bus (TLS, QUIC with certs, usrpwd, …). Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given (flag > env > context > file). A file that sets a session namespace is refused — explorers run un-namespaced (RFC 09 §5)
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

